 "question/problem" "value" "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-6832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-6832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/activity-metadata.json" "file_s