kers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327880" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9042a3bb57b43217cf1bb0cf5cc16f1106958207637db566b7d3aa09aea4bef8"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\le x\\le 13,\\,3\\le y\\le 7"] ["math" "-6\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 7"] ["math" "0\\le x\\le 5,\\,-7\\le y\\le 0"] ["math" "1\\le x\\le 5,\\,-9\\le y\\le -2"] ["math" "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}{{field-5}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9042a3bb57b43217cf1bb0cf5cc16f11