" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327884" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "q-327884" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "04ee7a68d2030797b5734e4b1aed9f05e0ee75f180ee5fd6ab516739b5a92b6b"} {"question" "e-5075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5075.json" "file_sha256" "71bb965abbdb12474c661d71e3d8d643d7e7e21db4f65a2df00aff965190352d"} {"question" "e-5075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5075.json" "file_sha256" "71bb965abbdb12474c661d71e3d8d643d7e7e21db4f65a2df00aff965190352d"} {"question" "e-5098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5098.json" "file_sha256" "24f8af9dc364ca16731d97aab77ebd8467eca182b9e9f838f2f7a6d108d62a25"} {"question" "e-5098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/assets/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5098.json" "file_sha256" "24f8af9dc364ca16731d97aab77ebd8467eca182b9e9f838f2f7a6d108d62a25"} {"question" "e-5099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5099.json" "file_sha256" "38577fa2a9b466481e5d94e70c1a41697903b87f9148d8c09e96870313c48a2a"} {"question" "e-5099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5099.json" "file_sha256" "38577fa2a9b466481e5d94e70c1a41697903b87f9148d8c09e96870313c48a2a"} {"question" "e-5292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5292.json" "file_sha256" "decad76cbea97f6b888b093020a4f553bae0f5ac2f293ca8074480d17953cebf"} {"question" "e-5292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/example-5292.json" "file_sha256" "decad76cbea97f6b888b093020a4f553bae0f5ac2f293ca8074480d17953cebf"} {"question" "q-16149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\ge -2"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "7166f97c080db241a5de02386a5c56f2af483cfccdda4ced22d1011a32453323" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Completing the square gives x=(t+1/2)^2-1/4, whose minimum -1/4 is attained at t=-1/2. Likewise y=t^2-2 has minimum -2 at t=0. Both variables are unbounded above and attain every value in their respective intervals. Thus original option 2 gives the exact separate ranges. Stored text '$x\\ge-\\frac14$ and $y\\ge-2$' agrees mathematically. This is a source-backed derived judgment, corroborated by the authentic solution and successful grading; preserve the original option's math value type." "bindings" {"problem" "d23609eb359baa1f174df562ed704cc76977b0192f8737db0b0653ea4a1065d4" "worked_solution" "db14ee9302940cff51aa462a33135afbfab1608337b797d7444f071e4a51e96c" "original_field" "f59ce100434aad59005c654a34ea380c4e201a569fcfac0b6224f94e654bef1f" "reviewed_field" "e2695e4f2f3bbf03035075790941530c4fbe1391181a152cc95b9895f6f16be0" "raw_widget" "402d156d300782585a3d695307192d076234f4fdf6c74458b881273e4ec4aaf0"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-16149-before.png" "sha256" "2b3ed679c4d38