 | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$"] [17592186261094 :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$"] [17592186261094 :question/answer-fields 17592186274880] [17592186263677 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=1-2t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-5$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-5$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/ef5f33b74b59b5a62fd8670a8feb6487b01a97aed4a44a098a576d10d891bd68.png)"] [17592186262743 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x={t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(5-t).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949104/assets/1aa848f287660c0858d5d6478da71c5baab9252c13f0a2d4a48c38d61f6bce51.png)"] [17592186087968 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nDetermine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n$x = t^{2} - 2, y = 3 - t,-3 \\le t \\le 3$"] [17592186258555 :question/answer-fields 17592186274508] [17592186087453 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$x=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3$,\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186087453 :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"] [17592186087457 :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$."] [17592186087457 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/803/Source/Images/e492a940fa95b406b60c6b0a5c438d98.png)"] [17592186087458 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?"] [17592186087458 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x & = {t}^{2} - 6 t \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - ( 3 {)}^{2} \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - 9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$"] [17592186087477 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?"] [17592186087477 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\cos   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  4 \\cos   \\theta \\le  4 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  x \\le  4.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$.\n- To find the range of $y$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\sin   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  2 \\sin   \\theta \\le  2 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  y \\le  2.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084820 17592186084821 17592186084822 17592186084823 17592186084824 17592186262745 17592186262746 17592186262747 17592186262748 17592186262749 17592186263658 17592186263659 17592186263660 17592186263661 17592186263662 17592186263679 17592186263680 17592186263681 17592186263682 17592186263683 17592186263709 17592186263711 17592186263713 17592186263716 17592186263717 17592186263719 17592186263721 17592186263734 17592186263735 17592186263737 17592186263739 17592186263741 17592186271158 17592186271159 17592186271160 17592186271161 17592186271162 17592186272983 17592186272984 17592186272985 17592186272986 17592186272987 17592186275693 17592186275695 17592186275697 17592186275699 17592186275701 17592186275703] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084819 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084819 :answer-field/id] [17592186084819 :answer-field/key] [17592186084819 :answer-field/type] [17592186262744 :answer-field/correct] [17592186262744 :answer-field/id] [17592186262744 :answer-field/key] [17592186262744 :answer-field/type] [17592186263657 :answer-field/correct] [17592186263657 :answer-field/id] [17592186263657 :answer-field/key] [17592186263657 :answer-field/type] [17592186263678 :answer-field/correct] [17592186263678 :answer-field/id] [17592186263678 :answer-field/key